unctions.","Only functions differentiable at every point."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4a2af066b242b8f92dce34b1","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiability_class_60b39faf_008_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $X$ be an open interval and $f\\in C^3(X)$. Which statement is guaranteed?","options":["$f\\in C^1(X)$","$f\\in C^4(X)$","$f'''$ is differentiable on $X$","$f'\\in C^3(X)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e417690c77c9d499fa016a13","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiability_class_60b39faf_009_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=x^2\\sin(1/x)$ for $x\\neq0$ and $f(0)=0$. Which statement about $f$ on $\\mathbb{R}$ is correct?","options":["$f$ is differentiable on $\\mathbb{R}$, but $f\\notin C^1(\\mathbb{R})$.","$f\\in C^1(\\mathbb{R})$, since $f'(x)$ exists at every $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.","$f$ is not differentiable at $0$, since $\\sin(1/x)$ has no limit at $0$.","$f\\notin C^0(\\mathbb{R})$, since $f$ oscillates infinitely often near $0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5a8104dadc905a7ba1e906f7","time_limit":45},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiability_class_60b39faf_011_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=x|x|$ on $\\mathbb{R}$. What is the largest integer $k\\ge0$ with $f\\in C^k(\\mathbb{R})$?","options":["$k=0$","$k=1$","$k=2$","There is none: $f\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb{R})$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b32f1fdf2b32e5eb2fc23cfe","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiability_class_60b39faf_012_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $X$ be an open interval and $f\\in C^1(X)$ with $f\\notin C^2(X)$. Which statement must be false?","options":["$f\\in C^0(X)$","$f'$ is continuous on $X$","$f\\in C^\\infty(X)$","$f'$ exists at every point of $X$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a838321631187e774c931249","time_limit":30},{"id":"expansion_pack_015_differentiability_class_new_006_curated20261027_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which function belongs to $C^\\infty(\\mathbb{R})$?","options":["$f(x)=|x|$","$f(x)=x|x|$","$f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$","$f(x)=x^5-3x+1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"65f512961946833e2be75ee4","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiability_class_60b39faf_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=|x|$. Which statement about differentiability classes of $f$ on open intervals is correct?","options":["$f\\in C^\\infty((0,\\infty))$, since $f(x)=x$ on that interval","$f\\notin C^1(X)$ for every open interval $X$, since $|x|$ has a corner","$f\\in C^1((-1,1))$, since $f'$ exists at all but one point there","$f\\in C^1((-1,1))$ once the value $f'(0)$ is defined to be $0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7a2a59ec166df235d64a60ec","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiability_class_60b39faf_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ on the closed interval $[0,1]$, where $C^k([0,1])$ means that the derivatives on $(0,1)$ extend continuously to both endpoints. Which statement is correct?","options":["$f\\in C^1([0,1])$, since $f'$ exists and is continuous on $(0,1)$","$f\\notin C^0([0,1])$, since $f$ is not differentiable at $0$","$f\\in C^1([0,1])$ with $f'(0)=+\\infty$, matching the vertical tangent","$f\\in C^0([0,1])$ but $f\\notin C^1([0,1])$, since $f'$ blows up near $0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"033936181a18d0a64b73a329","time_limit":45}]}}